# UPGRADE-12

# Customer-Facing SaaS Product Layer

# Self-Service Onboarding, Developer Portal,

# Public API & SDKs, Plans/Entitlements,

# Usage-Based Billing Adapter, Customer Webhooks,

# Usage/Cost UX and Enterprise Administration

You are operating directly inside ZCoder after Upgrade-11.

Current reported baseline includes:

* PostgreSQL 16 production backend
* verified tenant RLS
* Organization / Project model
* enterprise RBAC
* OIDC
* SCIM
* service accounts
* API keys
* policy engine
* immutable usage ledger
* atomic quotas
* enterprise audit
* regional/residency model
* GitHub App fleet
* autonomous job runtime
* CI/PR automation
* Kubernetes / Helm
* observability
* evidence-aware release gate
* 707 passing tests
* 2 optional UI skips
* release status PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS
* canonical version 1.40.0

Do not trust the report blindly.

Verify repository state first.

================================================================
MISSION
=======

Turn ZCoder's enterprise control-plane foundation into a coherent,
customer-facing SaaS product.

Target user journey:

SIGN UP
↓
CREATE ORGANIZATION
↓
CREATE PROJECT
↓
CHOOSE PLAN
↓
CONNECT GITHUB
↓
CONFIGURE MODEL / RUNTIME / POLICY
↓
CREATE FIRST JOB
↓
WATCH AGENT EXECUTION
↓
OPEN PR
↓
VIEW USAGE / COST
↓
CREATE API KEY / WEBHOOK
↓
AUTOMATE THROUGH PUBLIC API / SDK

The resulting product must support:

1. self-service onboarding
2. organizations/projects
3. GitHub App connection
4. plans and entitlements
5. trials
6. usage-based commercial billing boundary
7. customer usage dashboard
8. budget / quota controls
9. public REST API
10. API versioning
11. generated API documentation
12. Python SDK
13. TypeScript SDK
14. customer webhooks
15. service accounts/API keys
16. organization administration
17. enterprise SSO/SCIM configuration
18. developer portal
19. audit/security UI
20. support/admin tooling
21. lifecycle management
22. accurate release evidence

Do not build a pretty frontend over fake backend functions.

Every customer-facing capability must trace to real shared services.

================================================================
PHASE 0 — BASELINE & EVIDENCE AUDIT
===================================

Run:

git status --short
git log -30 --oneline
git tag --sort=-version:refname | head -30

python -m pytest -q -ra

python main.py --version
python main.py --help

python -m build

git diff --check

Run release gate.

Verify:

VERSION_INTEGRITY
TEST_ACCOUNTING
POSTGRES_REQUIRED
RLS
CROSS_TENANT
QUOTA_RACE
SECURITY

Do not proceed if a P0 tenant-isolation regression exists.

================================================================

1. PRODUCT DOMAIN BOUNDARY
   ================================================================

Separate:

control-plane domain

from:

commercial SaaS/product domain.

Introduce or consolidate concepts:

CustomerAccount
Organization
Project
Subscription
Plan
Entitlement
Trial
Usage
BillingProfile
PaymentProviderRef
DeveloperApplication
WebhookEndpoint

Do not put payment-provider IDs directly into unrelated domain models.

================================================================
2. USER VS PRINCIPAL
====================

Keep distinction between:

HumanUser
ServiceAccount
APIKeyPrincipal
PlatformAdmin

A customer account can contain multiple principals.

Do not model machine clients as users.

================================================================
3. CUSTOMER ACCOUNT
===================

Create first-class customer/account lifecycle where useful.

Potential states:

PENDING
ACTIVE
PAST_DUE
SUSPENDED
CANCELLED

Do not automatically suspend tenants solely because a third-party
payment webhook arrived without reconciliation.

================================================================
4. SELF-SERVICE SIGNUP
======================

Implement a secure onboarding flow.

Concept:

create account
→ verify identity
→ create organization
→ create default project
→ select plan/trial
→ connect GitHub
→ create first job

Every step should be resumable.

Do not leave partially initialized organizations.

================================================================
5. ONBOARDING STATE MACHINE
===========================

Represent onboarding state.

Example:

ACCOUNT_CREATED
EMAIL_OR_IDENTITY_VERIFIED
ORGANIZATION_CREATED
PLAN_SELECTED
GITHUB_CONNECTED
PROJECT_CONFIGURED
FIRST_JOB_CREATED
COMPLETED

Do not derive onboarding completion only from UI state.

================================================================
6. ONBOARDING IDEMPOTENCY
=========================

Page refresh/retry must not create:

duplicate organization
duplicate project
duplicate subscription
duplicate GitHub mapping

Use durable state and idempotent operations.

================================================================
7. TRIAL MODEL
==============

Support trial concept independently from payment provider.

Fields may include:

starts_at
ends_at
status
trial_entitlements

States:

ACTIVE
EXPIRED
CONVERTED
CANCELLED

Do not scatter:

if trial:

through application code.

Resolve through entitlement/billing services.

================================================================
8. PLANS
========

Define product plans independently from billing-provider objects.

Example conceptual tiers:

Free
Developer
Team
Business
Enterprise

Do NOT hardcode these exact names if repository/product requirements
already define another model.

Plan should reference versioned entitlement bundle.

================================================================
9. ENTITLEMENT SERVICE
======================

Every feature decision should use:

EntitlementService

Examples:

max_projects
max_repositories
monthly_agent_budget
concurrent_jobs
managed_agents
multiagent
SCIM
SSO
advanced_policy
audit_export
retention_days
regional_controls
priority_support

Avoid:

if plan == "enterprise"

in runtime code.

================================================================
10. ENTITLEMENT VERSIONING
==========================

Plan definitions change over time.

Persist entitlement version/reference associated with subscription.

Do not retroactively alter historical contract behavior without explicit
migration.

================================================================
11. ENTITLEMENT CACHE
=====================

If entitlement results are cached:

scope by organization
subscription version
policy version

Changes must invalidate safely.

Do not allow stale entitlement cache to grant revoked premium features
indefinitely.

================================================================
12. FEATURE GATING
==================

Feature gate flow:

authenticated principal
+
organization
+
subscription/entitlement
+
authorization policy

→ allow / deny / obligation

Entitlement is not authorization.

A paid plan must not override RBAC.

================================================================
13. PRODUCT CATALOG
===================

Create provider-neutral product catalog.

Concept:

Product
Plan
Price
MeterDefinition
EntitlementBundle

Separate:

commercial price

from:

provider/Anthropic cost.

================================================================
14. PRICE VERSIONING
====================

Pricing definitions need:

currency
billing interval
effective_from
effective_until
version

Do not mutate historical invoice meaning.

================================================================
15. MULTI-CURRENCY BOUNDARY
===========================

If only one currency is implemented:

say so.

Do not claim multi-currency because a currency field exists.

Architecture may support it later.

================================================================
16. BILLING PROVIDER INTERFACE
==============================

Create a provider-neutral interface.

Concept:

BillingProvider:
create_customer()
update_customer()
create_subscription()
change_subscription()
cancel_subscription()
report_usage()
preview_invoice()
get_invoice()
get_payment_status()
reconcile()
verify_webhook()

Do not make Stripe objects the domain model.

================================================================
17. STRIPE ADAPTER
==================

If Stripe is chosen as the first billing implementation:

implement it behind:

StripeBillingProvider

Do not make Stripe mandatory for local/self-hosted mode.

Support FakeBillingProvider for deterministic CI.

================================================================
18. BILLING CUSTOMER MAPPING
============================

Store:

ZCoder organization
↔
billing-provider customer reference

Mapping must be unique and tenant-scoped.

Never trust provider metadata alone as authorization.

================================================================
19. SUBSCRIPTION MAPPING
========================

Persist:

organization
internal subscription
provider subscription reference
plan version
status
period
provider sync timestamp

Do not reconstruct entire business state only from webhooks.

================================================================
20. SUBSCRIPTION STATES
=======================

Normalize provider states into ZCoder domain.

Example:

TRIALING
ACTIVE
PAST_DUE
PAUSED
CANCELLED
INCOMPLETE

Do not expose provider-specific state everywhere.

================================================================
21. BILLING RECONCILIATION
==========================

Webhooks are signals, not the only source of truth.

Implement reconciliation:

local subscription state
+
provider state
→ reconciled state

This must be idempotent.

================================================================
22. BILLING WEBHOOK SECURITY
============================

Verify signatures using the billing provider's current official
mechanism.

Persist delivery/event IDs.

Duplicate events must not:

create duplicate subscriptions
double-charge usage
change plan twice

================================================================
23. BILLING EVENT ORDERING
==========================

Provider webhook events may arrive:

late
duplicated
out of order

Reducers must be tolerant.

Store provider event timestamp + ingest timestamp.

Do not rely solely on arrival order.

================================================================
24. USAGE BILLING BOUNDARY
==========================

The existing immutable usage ledger remains authoritative for ZCoder
usage.

Billing provider receives normalized billable events derived from that
ledger.

Do not make provider meter event storage the only usage history.

================================================================
25. BILLABLE METERS
===================

Define explicit internal meters.

Examples:

agent_input_tokens
agent_output_tokens
managed_runtime_seconds
job_executions
premium_tool_usage
storage

Do not bill every metric merely because it exists.

Each meter requires product decision.

================================================================
26. METER MAPPING
=================

Map:

internal meter
→ provider meter

through configuration.

Persist provider meter reference.

Do not hardcode external IDs.

================================================================
27. USAGE EVENT IDEMPOTENCY
===========================

Each billable event must have stable identity.

Example:

usage_event_id

When exporting to billing provider:

retrying must never duplicate the billable quantity.

================================================================
28. BILLING OUTBOX
==================

Use durable outbox:

usage ledger transaction
→ enqueue billing export

Do not perform third-party billing call inside critical usage DB
transaction.

================================================================
29. BILLING EXPORT STATES
=========================

Use:

PENDING
DELIVERING
DELIVERED
RETRY
DEAD

Provide operator visibility.

================================================================
30. BILLING ADJUSTMENTS
=======================

Corrections must not rewrite usage history silently.

Use:

adjustment event

with:

original event reference
reason
actor
amount

Provider-specific adjustment capability may be used where supported.

================================================================
31. BILLING RECONCILIATION JOB
==============================

Periodically compare:

ZCoder aggregate
provider aggregate

Report discrepancies.

Do not auto-write financial corrections without policy.

================================================================
32. INVOICE PREVIEW
===================

Expose customer invoice estimate/preview where provider supports it.

Clearly identify:

estimate
draft
final

Do not label preview as final invoice.

================================================================
33. PAYMENT STATUS
==================

Surface:

current plan
next billing date
status
outstanding action

Avoid exposing sensitive payment instrument information unnecessarily.

================================================================
34. CHECKOUT / PAYMENT PORTAL
=============================

If using Stripe or another provider:

prefer provider-hosted secure checkout/customer portal where appropriate.

Do not collect raw card numbers in ZCoder unless there is a very strong
PCI-driven reason.

================================================================
35. BILLING FAILURE POLICY
==========================

Define what PAST_DUE means.

Possible behavior:

grace period
disable new premium jobs
preserve read access
allow billing/security admins
continue audit access

Do not instantly destroy running jobs.

================================================================
36. PLAN CHANGE
===============

Support:

upgrade
downgrade

Define when entitlements change:

immediately
next billing period
provider-confirmed

Make policy explicit.

================================================================
37. CANCELLATION
================

Support:

cancel immediately
cancel at period end

where product/provider supports it.

Do not delete tenant data on subscription cancellation.

================================================================
38. FREE / SELF-HOSTED MODE
===========================

Local/self-hosted usage must remain possible without a billing provider.

Separate:

commercial SaaS mode

from:

local/community deployment mode.

================================================================
39. PUBLIC API
==============

Create a stable customer API boundary.

Suggested namespace:

/api/v1/

Do not expose internal ORM/database objects.

================================================================
40. PUBLIC API RESOURCE MODEL
=============================

Core resources may include:

organizations
projects
repositories
jobs
job events
artifacts
approvals
API keys
service accounts
usage
quotas
webhooks
audit exports

Only expose resources justified for customer automation.

================================================================
41. OPENAPI
===========

Generate/maintain a formal OpenAPI specification.

Verify the current OpenAPI specification and local tooling compatibility.

Do not adopt a newer spec revision if code generators/tooling in this
repository do not support it reliably.

The API spec should be contract-tested against actual routes.

================================================================
42. API VERSIONING
==================

Define strategy before public release.

Possible:

path version

/api/v1/

Keep breaking changes out of a stable version.

Do not version merely internal implementation changes.

================================================================
43. RESOURCE IDENTIFIERS
========================

Use opaque stable IDs.

Do not expose sequential database PKs where avoidable.

Never rely on opaque IDs as authorization.

================================================================
44. PAGINATION
==============

All potentially large collections must be bounded.

Prefer cursor pagination for high-volume/event-style resources where
appropriate.

Document:

limit
cursor
next cursor

Do not permit unlimited list operations.

================================================================
45. FILTERS
===========

Allowlist filter/sort fields.

Never transform arbitrary customer input directly into SQL fragments.

================================================================
46. IDEMPOTENCY HEADER
======================

Important create/mutation endpoints should support an idempotency key.

Examples:

job creation
webhook registration
service-account creation
billing-sensitive commands

Store:

key
principal
endpoint/action
request fingerprint
result

Prevent reuse with different parameters.

================================================================
47. REQUEST IDS
===============

Every API response should provide:

request_id

Use it in logs/audit/tracing.

Never use request ID as authentication.

================================================================
48. API ERROR SCHEMA
====================

Standardize:

{
"error": {
"code": "...",
"message": "...",
"request_id": "...",
"details": ...
}
}

Do not expose traceback/SQL/provider secrets.

================================================================
49. RATE LIMIT RESPONSE
=======================

Public API should provide useful limit behavior.

Document:

HTTP status
retry metadata

Do not reveal cross-tenant usage.

================================================================
50. API AUTHENTICATION
======================

Support:

human/OIDC session where appropriate
API keys
service accounts

Keep authentication method independent from authorization.

================================================================
51. API KEY UX
==============

Customer portal:

create
name
scope
expiry
rotate
revoke
last used

Show full secret only once.

Never redisplay.

================================================================
52. DEVELOPER APPLICATION MODEL
===============================

If customer integrations need first-class application identity, create:

DeveloperApplication

with:

organization
name
credentials
webhooks
scopes

Do not prematurely implement OAuth authorization server functionality
unless there is a concrete need.

================================================================
53. CUSTOMER WEBHOOKS
=====================

Allow customers to register outbound webhook endpoints.

Events may include:

job.created
job.started
job.completed
job.failed
job.approval_required
job.budget_reached
pr.created
pr.merged
quota.warning

Do not expose internal-only events by default.

================================================================
54. WEBHOOK SUBSCRIPTIONS
=========================

Customer chooses event types.

Store:

endpoint
event subscriptions
status
secret reference/hash
created_at

================================================================
55. WEBHOOK SIGNATURES
======================

Sign webhook payloads.

Use established HMAC/signature design.

Include:

event ID
timestamp
payload

Prevent replay.

Document verification examples.

================================================================
56. WEBHOOK ROTATION
====================

Support webhook signing-secret rotation.

Allow safe overlap where needed.

Do not require downtime.

================================================================
57. WEBHOOK DELIVERY
====================

Persist deliveries.

Track:

PENDING
SUCCESS
RETRY
FAILED

Record HTTP status, duration and bounded response metadata.

Never store arbitrary huge customer response bodies.

================================================================
58. WEBHOOK RETRY
=================

Use bounded exponential backoff with jitter.

Avoid endless retry.

Dead deliveries must remain inspectable.

================================================================
59. WEBHOOK REPLAY
==================

Customer portal should allow authorized replay of historical event.

Replay must create a new delivery attempt while preserving original event
identity.

================================================================
60. WEBHOOK SSRF
================

Outbound customer webhooks are SSRF-sensitive.

Protect:

loopback
link-local
cloud metadata
private ranges according to deployment policy
redirect chains
DNS resolution behavior

Provide controlled allowlist exceptions for legitimate enterprise private
endpoints only when architecture supports them safely.

================================================================
61. PYTHON SDK
==============

Provide an official ZCoder Python SDK.

Do not hand-write hundreds of duplicated models if generation from
OpenAPI is reliable.

SDK should support:

client initialization
API key auth
pagination
idempotency
request IDs
typed errors
timeouts
retry policy

================================================================
62. TYPESCRIPT SDK
==================

Provide equivalent TypeScript/JavaScript SDK.

Support modern Node environments and appropriate browser restrictions.

Never expose service credentials to browser code.

================================================================
63. SDK VERSIONING
==================

SDK version is distinct from API version.

Document compatibility:

SDK version
API version

Do not tie them unnecessarily 1:1.

================================================================
64. SDK CONTRACT TESTS
======================

Use generated/fake server tests.

Prove SDKs can:

create job
get job
paginate events
handle errors
send idempotency key
verify request ID

================================================================
65. SDK RELEASE
===============

Build package artifacts.

Do not publish externally unless explicitly required.

Verify:

Python package build
Node package build

before claiming release-ready SDK.

================================================================
66. DEVELOPER PORTAL
====================

Create customer-facing developer experience.

Pages:

API overview
Authentication
Quickstart
API reference
SDKs
Webhooks
Errors
Rate limits
Idempotency
Examples

Docs must derive from actual API behavior.

================================================================
67. API EXPLORER
================

Optionally provide authenticated interactive API explorer.

Never expose secret API keys in generated static HTML or telemetry.

================================================================
68. QUICKSTART
==============

A developer should be able to:

create key
install SDK
submit first job
watch status
retrieve result

with minimal steps.

Quickstart must use actual tested commands.

================================================================
69. CUSTOMER DASHBOARD
======================

Build product UI around real APIs/services.

Core customer views:

Overview
Projects
Repositories
Jobs
Agents/Runtimes
Usage
Billing
API Keys
Webhooks
Members
SSO/SCIM
Policies
Audit
Settings

================================================================
70. OVERVIEW
============

Show useful data:

running jobs
recent failures
usage this period
budget remaining
connected repositories
PR activity

Avoid decorative fake metrics.

================================================================
71. JOB UX
==========

Provide:

submit task
choose project/repo
runtime selection where entitlement allows
budget
branch/base
policy preview

Then:

live timeline
tool/actions
approval
validation
PR
artifacts
cost

================================================================
72. FIRST-JOB EXPERIENCE
========================

After GitHub connection:

detect accessible repo
select repository
analyze project
recommend safe default validation
run low-risk first task

Do not automatically mutate repository during onboarding without clear
user action.

================================================================
73. APPROVAL UX
===============

Customer portal must clearly display:

action
risk
tool
arguments
repository
agent/job
policy reason

Allow:

approve once
deny

Broad approval needs explicit product/security policy.

================================================================
74. USAGE DASHBOARD
===================

Show:

current period
provider-reported usage
billable usage
estimated cost
quota
plan allowance
trend

Never present estimate as finalized invoice.

================================================================
75. USAGE BREAKDOWN
===================

Support authorized breakdown by:

project
repository
runtime
model
job
period

Use scalable aggregation rather than huge raw-query pages.

================================================================
76. BUDGET UX
=============

Customers configure:

organization limit
project limit
job limit
alerts

Explain distinction:

quota
budget
provider hard cap

================================================================
77. BILLING PAGE
================

Show:

plan
subscription state
billing period
usage
invoice preview/history where available
payment portal link where applicable

Do not expose provider implementation details unnecessarily.

================================================================
78. PLAN COMPARISON
===================

Plan UI must read current product catalog/entitlements.

Do not hardcode plan matrix separately in frontend.

================================================================
79. MEMBER ADMIN
================

Support:

invite
remove
role change
project access

Respect SSO/SCIM-managed organizations.

If SCIM owns membership:

UI should not fight authoritative IdP state.

================================================================
80. SSO/SCIM UI
===============

Enterprise settings:

configure OIDC
test configuration
SCIM token lifecycle
group mappings
status

Changing SSO must require elevated authorization.

================================================================
81. SECURITY PAGE
=================

Expose:

active API keys
service accounts
SSO
SCIM
webhooks
audit export
recent security events
data residency
retention

No raw secrets.

================================================================
82. AUDIT UX
============

Filter by:

actor
action
resource
result
time

Support export.

Pagination mandatory.

================================================================
83. ORGANIZATION SETTINGS
=========================

Manage:

name
billing
security
residency
retention
integrations

Project-specific settings remain project-scoped.

================================================================
84. PROJECT SETTINGS
====================

Manage:

repositories
agents
runtime
budget
policies
validation
webhooks where project-scoped

================================================================
85. GITHUB INSTALL FLOW
=======================

Customer portal should guide:

install GitHub App
select repositories
return to ZCoder
map repo to project
verify permission
show readiness

Never enable all installation repositories automatically without policy.

================================================================
86. GITHUB INSTALL CSRF / STATE
===============================

Use cryptographically secure state correlation.

Return from GitHub must map to intended authenticated organization.

Do not trust installation ID alone.

================================================================
87. GITHUB PERMISSION UX
========================

Explain required GitHub permissions.

Show missing permission errors clearly.

Do not silently request broader access.

================================================================
88. REPOSITORY READINESS
========================

For connected repository inspect:

default branch
required checks
merge policy
workflow support
agent config
security-sensitive paths

Display issues before autonomous jobs.

================================================================
89. PRODUCT NOTIFICATIONS
=========================

Introduce internal notification domain.

Possible events:

job failed
approval needed
quota warning
billing problem
GitHub disconnected
SCIM error

Delivery adapters may include:

in-app
email
webhook

Do not make Gmail or another provider part of domain model.

================================================================
90. NOTIFICATION PREFERENCES
============================

Users/organizations control allowed notification channels/events where
appropriate.

Security-critical notifications may be mandatory.

================================================================
91. EMAIL ADAPTER
=================

If email is implemented:

use a provider-neutral EmailProvider interface.

Do not run SMTP credentials through ordinary event payloads.

================================================================
92. INVITATIONS
===============

Organization invitation flow:

create invitation
send link
accept
expire
revoke

Token must be:

random
time-bounded
single-use or safely idempotent

Store only safe representation where possible.

================================================================
93. ACCOUNT RECOVERY
====================

If local account recovery exists:

design securely.

If authentication is entirely upstream OIDC:

do not invent password recovery.

================================================================
94. SUPPORT PORTAL BOUNDARY
===========================

Platform support tooling should allow:

search organization
view health/status
view billing synchronization
view integration state

Do not grant support automatic access to source code/job prompts.

================================================================
95. SUPPORT ACCESS
==================

Any exceptional customer-data access requires:

reason
role
audit
time scope

Do not create invisible impersonation.

================================================================
96. PLATFORM ADMIN PORTAL
=========================

Separate platform administration from customer portal.

Views:

organizations
subscriptions
billing reconciliation
fleet
workers
incidents
usage
provider health
audit/evidence

Strict global-admin authorization.

================================================================
97. TENANT IMPERSONATION
========================

Default: do not implement.

If essential:

explicit support session
customer/organization context
reason
duration
high-quality audit

Never silently impersonate.

================================================================
98. SEARCH
==========

Provide tenant-scoped search where useful.

Search index must contain tenant identity.

Cross-tenant results are P0 breach.

Do not launch complex search infrastructure unless needed.

================================================================
99. UX ERROR MODEL
==================

UI errors should map API error codes into actionable messages.

Never display raw provider/database stack trace.

================================================================
100. LOADING / RETRY UX
=======================

Long-running agent operations should show durable state.

Browser refresh must not lose job status.

Use server job state as source of truth.

================================================================
101. REALTIME UX
================

Use SSE/WebSocket shared service.

Reconnect from durable sequence/cursor.

Avoid duplicate timeline entries.

Tenant authorization remains mandatory.

================================================================
102. ACCESSIBILITY
==================

Customer UI should support reasonable keyboard and screen-reader
semantics.

Do not defer basic accessibility solely for visual polish.

================================================================
103. RESPONSIVE DESIGN
======================

Operations pages are desktop-heavy but must remain usable on smaller
screens where reasonable.

Do not sacrifice data density blindly.

================================================================
104. PRODUCT DESIGN SYSTEM
==========================

Create reusable components/tokens if frontend already supports a design
system.

Avoid copying style logic per page.

================================================================
105. FRONTEND API CLIENT
========================

Frontend consumes the public/internal authenticated API layer.

Do not call PostgreSQL or provider APIs directly from browser.

Never expose:

Anthropic API key
GitHub App private key
billing provider secret

to browser.

================================================================
106. CSRF / SESSION
===================

If cookie-authenticated mutation endpoints exist:

enforce appropriate CSRF protections.

Use secure:

HttpOnly
Secure
SameSite

settings according to deployment architecture.

================================================================
107. CSP / XSS
==============

Maintain CSP and safe output encoding.

Agent-generated text, GitHub comments and repository content are
untrusted.

Never render arbitrary HTML from agent output without sanitization.

================================================================
108. PROMPT CONTENT UX SECURITY
===============================

Agent/tool output may contain deceptive content.

Clearly distinguish:

system status
user input
agent output
external content

Do not let repository text render as privileged UI instruction.

================================================================
109. PUBLIC API SECURITY
========================

Audit against current OAuth/API security guidance.

Use exact redirect URI matching for OAuth-style integrations where
applicable.

Prefer modern authorization-code/PKCE patterns where browser OAuth
clients are introduced.

Do not implement deprecated implicit/password flows.

================================================================
110. CUSTOMER WEBHOOK SECURITY TESTS
====================================

Test:

valid signature
invalid signature
expired timestamp
duplicate event
redirect
private IP
DNS manipulation where feasible
slow endpoint
large response
timeout

================================================================
111. PUBLIC API IDOR TESTS
==========================

For every public resource:

Org A authenticated.

Guess Org B identifier.

Expected:

no data disclosure
no mutation

Test SDK and raw API.

================================================================
112. PUBLIC API RATE LIMITS
===========================

Test:

per-principal
per-organization

One noisy customer must not starve others.

================================================================
113. PUBLIC API IDEMPOTENCY TESTS
=================================

Same key + same request
→ same result

Same key + different request
→ error

Concurrent duplicate request
→ one logical operation

================================================================
114. BILLING TEST MATRIX
========================

Using FakeBillingProvider, test:

trial start
trial conversion
subscription create
upgrade
downgrade
cancel end-of-period
immediate cancel
past due
recovery
duplicate webhook
out-of-order webhook
provider timeout
reconciliation

No real financial charge in CI.

================================================================
115. STRIPE CONTRACT TESTS
==========================

If Stripe adapter is implemented:

mock at HTTP/SDK boundary.

Verify official:

request fields
idempotency
meter events
subscription/customer mapping
webhook verification

Expected values should be independent of implementation constants.

================================================================
116. OPTIONAL STRIPE SANDBOX TEST
=================================

Create separately marked:

pytest -m stripe_live

Use test mode only.

Never use production customer/account objects.

Default CI needs no Stripe credentials.

================================================================
117. BILLING USAGE E2E
======================

Offline required test:

create organization
→ subscribe plan
→ run FakeRuntime jobs
→ usage ledger entries
→ billing outbox
→ FakeBillingProvider receives meter events
→ invoice preview
→ usage dashboard

Prove no duplicate billing after event replay.

================================================================
118. PLAN ENTITLEMENT E2E
=========================

Test:

Free tenant requests premium capability
→ denied

Upgrade plan
→ entitlement becomes active

Downgrade
→ behavior follows configured timing

RBAC remains separately enforced.

================================================================
119. TRIAL E2E
==============

Create trial.

Run allowed jobs.

Expire trial.

Verify:

policy
usage
billing state
read access

No wall-clock sleeps; use injectable clock.

================================================================
120. ONBOARDING E2E
===================

Required deterministic scenario:

new principal
→ organization
→ project
→ plan/trial
→ fake GitHub install
→ repository mapping
→ create first job
→ FakeRuntime completes
→ PR created
→ usage updated

Verify persisted onboarding completion.

================================================================
121. DEVELOPER API E2E
======================

Create scoped API key.

Using Python SDK:

create job
poll/watch
retrieve artifacts

Using TypeScript SDK:

read same job or execute equivalent scenario.

Cross-tenant key fails.

================================================================
122. CUSTOMER WEBHOOK E2E
=========================

Register fake webhook endpoint.

Run job.

Receive:

job.started
job.completed

Verify signature.

Replay delivery.

Ensure business event not duplicated.

================================================================
123. CLEAN FRONTEND TEST
========================

Test primary workflows at component/integration level.

If browser E2E stack exists, cover:

signup/onboarding
GitHub connection
first job
approval
usage
API key
webhook
billing

Do not add fragile browser automation merely for test count.

================================================================
124. FRONTEND OPTIONAL DEPENDENCIES
===================================

Upgrade-11 still had optional TUI/Web skips.

If customer SaaS Web UI is now core product:

web tests can no longer be classified as optional in SaaS release CI.

Reclassify.

Local CLI release may keep UI optional.

Product release profile must require Web.

================================================================
125. RELEASE PROFILES
=====================

Separate release gates:

CORE_CLI
SELF_HOSTED
SAAS

Example:

CORE_CLI may not require billing/web.

SAAS must require:

WEB
POSTGRES
TENANCY
BILLING_BOUNDARY
PUBLIC_API
CUSTOMER_WEBHOOKS

Do not pretend one deployment profile has every feature.

================================================================
126. PRODUCT ANALYTICS
======================

If product analytics is implemented:

collect only necessary event metadata.

Do not send:

source code
agent prompts
secret values

to analytics by default.

================================================================
127. PRIVACY SETTINGS
=====================

Allow deployment/customer policy to control telemetry where appropriate.

Operational/security audit telemetry remains separate from product
analytics.

================================================================
128. DATA EXPORT
================

Provide customer export of appropriate:

organization config
projects
usage
audit
job metadata

Do not export secret plaintext.

================================================================
129. ACCOUNT / TENANT DELETION UX
=================================

Expose asynchronous deletion workflow.

Show:

requested
retention wait
deleting
complete

Billing cancellation and data deletion are distinct.

================================================================
130. BILLING DATA RETENTION
===========================

Financial records may have retention requirements distinct from agent
job data.

Model separately.

Do not automatically erase invoice/accounting references when project
data is removed.

================================================================
131. TERMS / PRIVACY BOUNDARY
=============================

Add product configuration/routes for:

Terms
Privacy
Security

Do not invent legal claims.

Mark templates for legal review.

================================================================
132. STATUS / INCIDENT BOUNDARY
===============================

Prepare customer-visible service health.

Do not automatically expose internal infrastructure details.

Map internal incidents to customer-impact states.

================================================================
133. PRODUCT HEALTH
===================

Expose customer-relevant integration health:

GitHub
agent runtime
billing
webhooks

Do not reveal another tenant's provider state.

================================================================
134. DEVELOPER PORTAL SEARCH
============================

Search docs only within public documentation.

Do not index private customer resources into docs search.

================================================================
135. CHANGELOG
==============

Create customer-facing API/product changelog separate from internal
commit history where helpful.

Highlight breaking/deprecation timelines.

================================================================
136. API DEPRECATION
====================

Public API requires explicit deprecation policy.

Do not delete fields/endpoints abruptly after launch.

Document:

deprecated_since
replacement
removal target

================================================================
137. SDK DEPRECATION
====================

SDK should expose deprecated API warnings/documentation without
inventing incompatible behavior.

================================================================
138. API CONTRACT SNAPSHOT
==========================

Store OpenAPI contract in repository.

CI must detect accidental breaking changes.

Use automated diff tooling where reliable.

Human review required for public breaking changes.

================================================================
139. API COMPATIBILITY GATE
===========================

Add:

PUBLIC_API_COMPAT

Release fails on unapproved breaking change.

================================================================
140. BILLING CONTRACT GATE
==========================

Add:

BILLING_LEDGER
BILLING_IDEMPOTENCY
BILLING_RECONCILIATION

No SaaS release if usage can double-charge.

================================================================
141. ENTITLEMENT GATE
=====================

Add:

ENTITLEMENTS

Test plan-feature matrix.

No customer should receive feature solely due to frontend state.

================================================================
142. WEBHOOK GATE
=================

Add:

CUSTOMER_WEBHOOKS

Must prove:

signature
replay safety
idempotent delivery
SSRF defense
tenant scoping

================================================================
143. SDK GATE
=============

Add:

PYTHON_SDK
TYPESCRIPT_SDK

Gate means:

build
install
smoke test
contract test

Not necessarily external package publication.

================================================================
